# Hypothesis — prevalence of gardening-introduced non-native plants by habitat type

Garden-introduced non-native plants in the Barcelona province plot network are concentrated in low-elevation,
human-built and hydric habitats and are nearly absent from upland semi-natural habitats. Prevalence is defined as
the fraction of sampled plots in a habitat that contain >= 1 gardening-introduced species (all sampled plots = denominator).

Wetlands 50.0% (19/38, 95% CI 34.8-65.2) and Urban 47.7% (31/65, 36.0-59.6) are highest, followed by Coastal 44.8%
(13/29, 28.4-62.5), Riparian 36.1% (22/61, 25.2-48.6) and Croplands 20.7% (19/92, 13.6-30.0); the lower end is
Meadows 7.7% (3/39), Coniferous forests 6.5% (5/77), Broad-leaved forests 4.4% (3/68), Scrublands 1.9% (2/106) and
Rock outcrops 0% (0/57). The four informative lowland/disturbed habitats pool to 44.0% (85/193) versus 6.0% (11/184)
in the semi-natural forest/meadow set: risk difference 38.1 percentage points (95% CI 30.3-45.9), risk ratio 7.4x,
odds ratio 12.4 (6.3-24.2), Fisher p = 9e-19.

Habitat x pathway heterogeneity in plot occupancy is significant by Monte-Carlo permutation (chi2 = 139.4, df = 9,
10,000 permutations, seed 20261023, p <= 0.0001; asymptotic p = 1.3e-25; no expected cell < 5), and the direction is
unchanged when restricted to the five well-sampled habitats (Coastal, Croplands, Riparian, Urban, Wetlands;
permutation p = 0.002) or when sampling effort is rarefied to 29 plots per habitat (identical rank order, rho = 1.00).

The gradient tracks propagule pressure / disturbance rather than habitat richness per se: gardening occupancy falls
monotonically with elevation (37.3% in the lowest quartile vs 3.2% in the highest; Spearman rho = -0.37), rises with
urban cover in a 1 km buffer (rho = +0.38) and with proximity to roads (rho = -0.19). In an elevation-adjusted logistic
model (plot as unit, Rock outcrops and Scrublands dropped for separation) Wetlands, Urban, Coastal and Riparian plots
still have 8-10x the odds of holding a garden-origin species relative to broad-leaved forest (all p <= 0.004), while
Coniferous forests show no elevation-adjusted difference (logit 0.32, p = 0.68).

Region-wide context (not estimated from the 632 plots) explains why gardening dominates the pool at all: gardening
introductions rose 18 -> 54 -> 142 -> 397 species across the four periods, reaching 75% of post-1985 introductions,
yet gardening species occupy fewer habitat types on average (mean habitat.range 1.95) than unintentional ones (2.77)
and more than AgriForest (1.56).

HYPOTHESIS: because the gardening pathway supplies most recent propagules and gardening ornamentals are preferentially
released and re-dispersed around human settlement, water bodies and riparian corridors, garden-introduced species will
show the highest plot-level prevalence in Urban, Wetlands, Coastal and Riparian habitats (roughly 4-5 plots in 10) and
the lowest in upland semi-natural habitats (Forests, Meadows, Scrublands, Rock outcrops: <= 8 plots in 100), an
approximate 7-12x contrast that is mediated by elevation and urban cover and that persists after elevation adjustment.

Caveat on prevalence definitions: the alternative, occurrence-conditioned view (sum n.gard / sum n.total) ranks
Broad-leaved (100%, 4/4 records) and Coniferous forests (87.5%, 7/8) first, but those denominators are 4 and 8 records
because those habitats host almost no unintentional or AgriForest colonisers; the two views are essentially
uncorrelated (Spearman rho = 0.08) and must not be blended.
